From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 12:40:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 19/38] fix(refinery): make find-work see in_progress beads on
 its own hook (gcp-s14g)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mol-refinery-patrol's find-work step scanned with --status=open, so a bead
that was in_progress, assigned to the refinery, and carrying metadata.branch
was invisible to the merge queue forever. The startup orphan scan — the
fallback that exists for exactly the beads the patrol query misses — filtered
--status=open too, so it did not cover the gap either.

The resulting stall is self-concealing, which is why it went unnoticed for as
long as it did: each patrol legitimately concludes "no work", writes
"IDLE: no work, exiting turn.", and exits, so no wake signal fires and the
queue looks healthy from outside. One bead sat ~19h and gated a nine-bead
wave; the merge landed in one try once the bead was actually seen. The
mitigation in force was a planning seat hand-checking the refinery's query
after every polecat submit, which only works while that seat is awake.

The invisible state is not the crash-recovery edge case the original analysis
framed it as. It arises on the NORMAL polecat submit path: the polecat
reassigns the bead to the refinery and the status does not always come back to
open. So both scans now sweep --status=open,in_progress. Re-entering a bead
the refinery already claimed is safe because merge-push discriminates before
it acts — the already-merged short-circuit (ancestor + fork_sha commit count)
closes a branch that previously landed, and the false-completion guard refuses
a branch that introduces no change. Status was never what protected that path;
it was only hiding the work. The assignee and has-metadata-key=branch filters,
not the status filter, are what keep the refinery out of a polecat's live
work.

The bead's second suggested fix — normalise status at handoff — was already
written into mol-polecat-work step 6 as --status=open and is precisely the
write observed not to stick. It now reads the status back and corrects it
once, so a bead does not sit in_progress with nobody working it, and warns
rather than aborting if it still will not settle: the branch is already pushed
by then, and the widened find-work scan means an unsettled status can no
longer hide the bead. Fixing only the handoff would have left the larger hole
open, so the queue-side fix is the load-bearing one.

Validation: gastown/tests/test_refinery_find_work.sh extracts the shipped
query text from the formula between sentinels and EXECUTES it against a stub
gc bd list, so the regression shows up as "the in_progress bead was not found"
rather than as a flag-string grep that proves nothing about which beads come
back. Verified red against the pre-fix query text (the in_progress case fails,
the open case still passes) and green after. Full gastown shell suite, go
vet/build/test -race, golangci-lint and validate_registry.py all pass.

Discovered, not fixed here: tests/test_no_bare_bd_commands.py fails on a clean
integration checkout (bare `bd` strings inside test_merge_approval_gate.sh's gc
stub) — filed as gcp-nduz.
